config.prompt:
Understand this goal and decompose it before doing any of it.

Read enough of {{inputs.scope}} that your plan describes what is actually there. A plan built on what would be convenient costs more than no plan, because the first cycle spends itself discovering the plan was wrong.

Goal:
{{inputs.task}}

Success criteria:
{{inputs.success_criteria}}

Note for later: an independent verifier will re-read these artifacts and re-run any command you cite. It will not see this message or anything you say about your own work — only the user's goal, the tool calls, and their output. Plan on being checked.

Return JSON: {"sub_goals": [string], "execution_plan": [{"phase": string, "objective": string}], "first_step": string}

Do ONE meaningful step toward the goal, then stop and report.

One step, not the whole plan: the cycle is judged, and a step that bundles four changes gives the judge nothing it can accept or reject cleanly.

Goal:
{{inputs.task}}

Plan:
{{nodes.intake.output.execution_plan}}

What previous cycles found:
{{last.output.summary}}

Report with evidence a verifier can check independently — file paths it can read, commands it can re-run with their actual output. "I improved the handler" is not evidence. "handlers.py:88 now short-circuits on empty input; pytest tests/test_handlers.py passes 12/12" is.

Set new_findings_count to the number of genuinely NEW things this cycle established. Zero is a valid and useful answer — it is how the loop learns it is done. Inflating it to look productive keeps the loop running past the point of value, which costs the user real money.

Return JSON: {"summary": string, "key_insight": string, "new_findings_count": integer, "evidence": string}

Synthesize what the cycles established into one deliverable the user can act on.

Goal:
{{inputs.task}}

Everything the cycles found:
{{nodes.work.output}}

Say plainly what you could NOT establish. A report that quietly omits its gaps reads as more complete than it is, and the reader has no way to know which parts to trust.

Return JSON: {"report": string, "key_findings": [string], "recommendations": [string], "could_not_establish": [string]}

Terminal acceptance: does this deliverable meet the goal?

Do not talk yourself into approving. This is the LAST check before the run reports success to the user — if you wave it through, nothing downstream catches it.

This is the last check before the run reports success, and three independent samples of you are being asked — a single judgement on a terminal accept was measured to be indistinguishable from noise.

Goal:
{{inputs.task}}

Success criteria:
{{inputs.success_criteria}}

Deliverable:
{{nodes.deliverable.output.report}}

What it admits it could not establish:
{{nodes.deliverable.output.could_not_establish}}

An honest gap is not a failure — a gap the deliverable hides is. Judge the work against the goal as stated, not against a better goal you would have set.

Return JSON: {"reasoning": string, "verdict": "PASS"|"REJECT", "scores": {criterion: integer}, "proof": string, "evidence_refs": [string], "cannot_judge": string}
